(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).asep=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.asep.full||[]).push({
  num: 3, part: `Part One: Concept`, title: `Good Requirements`, weeks: `Weeks 4 to 6`,
  tasks: [`T3`,`T9`,`M5`,`M6`,`X3`,`X2`,`X1`,`M1`,`O1`,`O6`],
  cast: [
    [`Beto Salinas`,`Nadia's old bench partner at the avionics shop in Colorado Springs. Still there. Still teasing her about how much she loves a green PASS.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Somebody had written 133 on the whiteboard in the HEARTH room in red marker, and nobody erased it. By Monday of week five Wes had drawn a box around it. Nobody asked him why.`,
    `The number sat over everyone's head while Jun printed the first draft of the system requirements. It came to four hundred lines and nineteen pages, and he carried it to Nadia's desk with both hands like something that might spill.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"It's everything,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Every stakeholder requirement, turned into what the module has to do. I didn't sleep much."</span>`,
    `Nadia started reading at her desk and finished in the high bay, sitting on an equipment case at the edge of the blue tape, with a red pen she hadn't meant to uncap. Some of it was very good. Line forty one said the cabin shall be quiet. Line ninety tried to do the whole hatch in one sentence. Line two hundred and six said the module shall be safe.`,
    `She could hear Colleen already. You can't fly a line that says be safe.`,
    `The system requirements review was at the end of week six. After that, these four hundred lines would be the baseline, the thing every drawing, every test and every argument for the next two and a half years would point back to. She uncapped the pen the rest of the way.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Colleen's Flight Notes: write it so it can be checked`,
    storyAt: 5, // Story only: her Thursday plays before f3s6 (Helen's Thursday), after the Tuesday with Jun
    sections: [
      { h: `From what they need to what it must do`,
        body: [`<!--story-->Colleen came in Thursday for the advisory board and stayed after, in the chair next to Nadia's desk, with her own red pen and Jun's draft between them. She'd read the whole thing the night before and circled the good lines too, which Nadia hadn't expected.`,
          `System requirements definition takes the stakeholder requirements, which are written from the user's side, and turns them into a technical view of what the system itself must do, how well, and under what constraints, without designing it. Its inputs are the stakeholder requirements, the operational concept and its scenarios, the MOEs, the constraints like volume and launch mass, and what's known about the interfaces. Its outputs are the system requirements, a verification method for each, the measures of performance that matter most, traceability up and down, and, at the system requirements review, the requirements baseline. Architecture definition uses them next. So do design, verification, and configuration management, which takes custody of the baseline.`,
          `<!--story--><span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Plain, exact, kind.">"A requirement is a promise you can check,"</span> Colleen said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Plain, exact, kind.">"If you can't check it, it's a wish."</span>`],
        terms: [[`System requirements definition`,`The process that turns stakeholder requirements into a technical view of what the system must do, with verification methods and traceability, ready to baseline.`],[`System requirement`,`A statement of what the system must do or how well, under stated conditions, that can be verified.`]],
        exam: `Stakeholder requirements are the user's view. System requirements are the system's view. System requirements get verified, and they're the main input to architecture definition.` },
      { h: `What makes one good`,
        body: [`<!--story-->She drew a line through "the cabin shall be quiet." <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, exact, a little wry.">"A checklist item that says check the engine is useless,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, exact, a little wry.">"Oil pressure in the green arc, I can do in the dark."</span>`,
          `A good requirement is necessary: something real breaks or goes missing without it. It's appropriate: written at the right level for this system, saying what, not how. It's unambiguous: only one reasonable reading. It's complete: it stands on its own without another document to explain it. It's singular: one requirement, one thing, so it can pass or fail on its own. It's feasible: it can be met within cost, schedule, technology and physics. It's verifiable: there's a way to show it was met. It's correct: it says what the need actually is. And it's conforming: it follows the project's agreed pattern and style. Traceability isn't on that list. It's an attribute every requirement carries, a link up to its parent and source, and it comes up again later as a method. The whole set should be complete and consistent too, with no gaps and no two requirements fighting each other. Jun's draft broke most of these somewhere, which is normal for a first draft. The point of a shared list is that a review argues about the module, not about taste.`,
          `<prop say="Line 41 of the system requirements, before and after. Before, the cabin shall be quiet. After, as S R 41: with all HEARTH systems in their nominal sleep period mode, the continuous noise level at the head position of each sleep station shall not exceed 45 decibels, A weighted. Its parent is S T K 12, crew sleep, and it's verified by test." type="doc" title="HEARTH system requirements, line 41, before and after">Before: The cabin shall be quiet.<br>After: SR-041. With all HEARTH systems in their nominal sleep period mode, the continuous noise level at the head position of each sleep station shall not exceed 45 dBA. Parent: STK-012 (crew sleep). Verification: test.</prop>`],
        terms: [[`Requirement characteristics`,`Necessary, appropriate, unambiguous, complete, singular, feasible, verifiable, correct, conforming. The set as a whole should be complete, consistent, feasible, comprehensible and able to be validated. Traceability is an attribute each requirement carries, not one of the nine.`]],
        exam: `Words like quick, easy, adequate, reliable, user friendly and safe, with no number or condition, are the exam's favorite signs of an unverifiable or ambiguous requirement. Two "shalls" or an "and" joining two behaviors means it isn't singular.` },
      { h: `Four ways to prove it`,
        body: [`Every requirement gets a verification method when it's written, not when the test campaign starts. There are four main ones. Inspection is looking: the label is there, the part number matches, the dimension is right. Analysis is calculation, modeling and simulation, for things you can't fully test, like fifteen years of micrometeoroids. Demonstration is operating the system and watching that it does the thing, without instrumented measurements: a crew member opens the hatch without tools. Test is operating it under controlled conditions and measuring: the leak rate at pressure, the noise at the pillow. You'll also see analogy or similarity, using evidence from an item already verified under the same conditions, but the four carry almost every program.`,
          `<!--story--><span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, absorbed, practical.">"Test is the one testers love,"</span> Colleen said, not looking up. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Dry, absorbed, practical.">"It's also the most expensive. Pick the one that gives you real evidence for that requirement, and nothing fancier."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Inspection`,`Verifying by looking at the item or its documents, without operating it.`],[`Analysis`,`Verifying by calculation, models or simulation, often backed by test data on samples.`],[`Demonstration`,`Verifying by operating the system and observing that it works, without instrumented measurement.`],[`Test`,`Verifying by operating the system under controlled conditions and measuring the result.`]],
        exam: `Match the method to the evidence. Present or marked: inspection. Can't be tested in full: analysis. Observed operation, pass or fail: demonstration. Measured result under controlled conditions: test.` },
      { h: `Trace it, then freeze it`,
        body: [`Traceability is a method, not a spreadsheet. Each system requirement links up to the stakeholder requirement or analysis it came from, and down to its verification and the parts of the design that meet it. Done in both directions, it tells you when a requirement has no parent, an orphan that may not be necessary, and when a need has no requirement under it, a gap. It's also how you find everything a change will touch. Jun's model can hold those links, which is the start of a digital thread through the program.`,
          `At the system requirements review, the set becomes the requirements baseline. From then on configuration management controls it: a change needs a change request, an impact assessment, and a decision by the board that owns the baseline. Open values marked to be determined are allowed in a baseline, if each one has an owner and a date. Configuration management controls changes to the baselined items. Information management is the wider job: getting the right information to the right people, current, protected and with the right access, and keeping the record of it.`,
          `<!--story-->Colleen tapped the margin where Nadia had written Wes's name twice. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Quiet, serious, kind.">"Watch the requirements nobody writes down,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Colleen" data-tone="Quiet, serious, kind.">"Wes has thirty years of rules in his head. Every one of them is probably right, and none of them is in your baseline. An organization that keeps its best knowledge in one man's head loses it the day he retires."</span>`,
          `Getting that kind of knowledge out of people and into a form the next program can use is the job of knowledge management, and it'll come up again.`],
        terms: [[`Traceability`,`Links from each requirement up to its source and down to its verification and design, used to find orphans, gaps, and the impact of changes.`],[`Requirements baseline`,`The approved set of requirements, set at the system requirements review and changed only through configuration control.`]],
        exam: `Orphan requirements may not be necessary. Needs with no requirements under them are gaps. After the baseline, every change goes through change control with an impact assessment.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f3s1`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T3`, title:`Four hundred lines`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday Nadia sat down with Jun and the red-marked draft. He looked at the ink and then at her.`,(G.skippedconops
        ?`A lot of the lines had no scenario behind them. They'd been written from notes in week two, before there was an operations concept, and some of them traced to nothing but a conversation.`
        :`Most of the lines traced to a scenario on the tape. Some of the language, though, would never survive a test engineer.`),`<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"How bad is it?"</span> he asked, and waited with his pen capped. What does Nadia do with the draft?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rewrite the forty worst lines herself tonight and hand Jun a clean draft.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The forty lines are much better. Jun doesn't know why, and the next four hundred he writes have the same problems.`,
          why:`Rewriting is faster than teaching, once. Requirements quality comes from everybody writing to the same characteristics, so the fix has to reach the writer.`},
        {t:`Walk ten lines with Jun against the characteristics, then let him finish.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`They take ten lines together, slowly: is it necessary, is there one reading, can it be checked, is it one thing, can it be built, where did it come from. By the twentieth line Jun is finding the problems before she does.`,
          why:`The requirement characteristics are a shared standard the whole team can apply. Reviewing against them, and teaching them, fixes the set and the people who'll keep writing it.`},
        {t:`Send the draft to Halo's reviewers this week and collect their comments.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Halo's reviewers mark the same vague words she did, plus some they'd prefer, and FRO looks like it didn't check its own work.`,
          why:`Customer review matters, after you've done your own quality pass. Sending a set you know is weak spends the customer's attention on problems you could have fixed.`},
        {t:`Accept the draft for now and tighten the wording later, during design.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Design starts against "the cabin shall be quiet." Three engineers pick three different noise limits.`,
          why:`Vague requirements don't get sharper during design. They get interpreted, differently, by each person who reads them. Fix them before the baseline.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s2`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T3`, title:`And seal reliably`, q:`Jun's happy with line ninety as it stands. What should Nadia do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Line ninety was the hatch. "The hatch shall open within 10 seconds and seal to the leak limit and be operable by one crew member from either side."`,`<span class="said" data-who="Jun">"It's all about the hatch,"</span> Jun said. <span class="said" data-who="Jun">"Isn't it cleaner in one place?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep line ninety as written, a single requirement for a single component.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`At verification the hatch opens in eight seconds and leaks a little high. Is line ninety met? Half yes, which on a verification matrix means no, and the record can't say which half failed.`,
          why:`A requirement with several behaviors can't pass or fail cleanly. Singular requirements can each be verified, traced, and changed on their own.`},
        {t:`Split line ninety into three requirements, one for each hatch behavior.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia has him split it into one requirement for each thing the hatch does. Three lines, three parents, three verification methods: test for the opening time, test for the leak rate, demonstration for one person operation. Jun renumbers them and looks pleased about it.`,
          why:`Singular means one requirement states one thing. It costs a few lines and gets you clean verification, clean traceability, and changes that touch only what they should.`},
        {t:`Keep it as one line, with a margin note that it covers three things.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The note helps whoever reads it carefully. The verification matrix still has one row for three behaviors.`,
          why:`Flagging the problem is better than hiding it. The fix is to make each requirement singular, so the tools and the people tracking it see three items.`},
        {t:`Move the details into the hatch spec and keep "the hatch shall work properly."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`The system requirement is now unverifiable, and the real requirements live in a design document that can change without anyone asking.`,
          why:`Pushing requirements into design hides them from the baseline. "Work properly" can't be verified at any level.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X3`, title:`Where did this come from`, q:`Wes wants a spreadsheet per subsystem, and Jun wants every line linked to its source. Where should traceability live?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wes wanted the requirements in a spreadsheet, the way FRO had always kept them, one tab per subsystem. Jun wanted them in the model, linked to the stakeholder needs and the scenarios.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"I can read a spreadsheet,"</span> Wes said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Everybody can read a spreadsheet."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep Wes's spreadsheet and add a column for each line's parent requirement.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It works, mostly. The parent column is typed by hand, and in a month eleven rows point at stakeholder requirements that were renumbered.`,
          why:`Recording parents is the right idea. Links typed by hand break silently, and a spreadsheet can't tell you what a change will ripple into.`},
        {t:`Keep the links in the model and generate a spreadsheet view for Wes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia keeps the links in Jun's model and has it print a spreadsheet view for Wes. Jun runs the first trace report that afternoon. Twelve requirements have no parent. Four needs have no requirement under them at all. Wes reads the spreadsheet view and says it looks like a spreadsheet, which is as close to approval as he gets.`,
          why:`Traceability is a method: links up to the source and down to verification, kept in both directions, so you can find orphans, gaps, and the reach of a change. A model holds the links. A view gives everyone access.`},
        {t:`Use Wes's spreadsheet now and add traceability once the set settles.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Later turns out to be after the baseline. By then nobody remembers where line two hundred came from.`,
          why:`Trace is cheapest when the requirement is written and its source is fresh. Adding it later means reconstructing rationale that's already gone.`},
        {t:`Trace each requirement down to its verification and skip the upward links.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Every line has a test. Nobody can say which ones the crew actually needs.`,
          why:`Downward trace shows how you'll verify. Upward trace shows why the requirement exists. Without both, you can't find unnecessary requirements or missing ones.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s4`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T3`, title:`Quiet enough to sleep`, q:`Line forty one says only that the cabin shall be quiet. What should Nadia do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Line forty one, the cabin shall be quiet, came from a stakeholder requirement about crew sleep. Helen's notes said the old station was so loud that some crews slept with earplugs every night, and under that, underlined: if it's quiet, they don't need earplugs, and they hear the alarm.`,`Tiago read it over Nadia's shoulder. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"How do I test quiet?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Reword it as "as quiet as practical" to leave room for trades.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The designers love it. Tiago asks how he tests practical.`,
          why:`"As practical" sounds flexible and can't be verified. Room to trade belongs in the analysis that sets the number, not in vague wording.`},
        {t:`Ask Helen to define quiet and record her answer under the requirement.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Helen gives a good answer and a number. It lives in a note, so the requirement still says quiet.`,
          why:`Going to the stakeholder is right. Her answer belongs in the requirement itself, as a measurable value with conditions.`},
        {t:`Set a noise limit in dBA at each bunk's head position in sleep mode.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They set forty five decibels, A weighted, at the head of each sleep station, with the module in its sleep mode, and trace it to crew sleep. Tiago nods. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"That I can put a microphone on."</span>`,
          why:`A verifiable requirement says what's measured, where, under what conditions, and to what limit. Now there's one reading and one way to prove it.`},
        {t:`Drop the line and leave noise to the air system's design rules.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The air system team designs to its own rules, which were written for cargo modules no one ever slept in.`,
          why:`A requirement with a real stakeholder need behind it is necessary. Leaving it to design rules means nobody owns the crew's sleep.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s5`, domain:`Cross-cutting`, task:`X2`, title:`Three things to open it`, q:`Odette's first hazard is the hatch coming open by accident on a pressurized module. What should the requirement say?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Odette came to Wednesday's requirements meeting with a hazard list and one page of proposed safety requirements. The first hazard was the hatch opening by accident while the module was pressurized.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Odette">"That one's catastrophic,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Odette">"So. What has to break for that hatch to open with air behind it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write that the hatch shall not open inadvertently and leave controls to design.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's a true statement of the goal. It doesn't say how many failures it has to survive, so it can't be verified and design has no target.`,
          why:`Safety requirements need to be verifiable like any other. State the hazard control and the fault tolerance, not just the wish.`},
        {t:`Take Odette's page as written for the hatch safety requirements.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Two of her requirements overlap with ones Jun already has, worded differently. Nobody notices until verification counts them twice.`,
          why:`Safety engineering brings the hazards and controls. Systems engineering integrates them into one consistent, traced set, so nothing conflicts or duplicates.`},
        {t:`Require three independent inhibits against opening under pressure.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The requirement Nadia writes calls for three independent inhibits against the hatch opening under pressure. Jun links it to the hazard and gives it a verification method. Odette reads the wording, changes one word, and initials it.`,
          why:`Safety requirements come from hazard analysis and control specific hazards. For a catastrophic hazard on a crewed system, that means fault tolerance stated in the requirement, traced to the hazard report it closes.`},
        {t:`Hold the safety requirements until the hazard analysis is done after PDR.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Design goes ahead with one hatch latch. Adding two more later costs a redesign of the hatch frame.`,
          why:`Safety requirements shape the design. Waiting for the design to be done before writing them guarantees rework.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s6`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T3`, title:`Sixty seconds`, q:`Helen crossed out the egress value and wrote sixty seconds. What should Nadia do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Helen's comments came back Thursday. On the egress requirement she'd crossed out the value and written 60 seconds, and under it, smaller: that's as long as I can watch the clock.`,`Jun pulled up the tape times. It took forty five seconds just to wake up and get out of a bunk.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write 60 seconds as Helen asked, as a hard target for the design.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`It goes in. At the review, Jun's own data shows it can't be done from bed, and the requirement either gets waived or ignored.`,
          why:`A requirement nobody can meet isn't ambitious. It's infeasible, and it teaches the program to ignore requirements.`},
        {t:`Walk Helen through the wake times and agree a value some layout can meet.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia calls Helen and walks her through the wake times, bunk by bunk. Helen looks at them for a long minute. <span class="said" data-who="Helen" data-tone="Quiet, plain, resigned.">"I know,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Helen">"I wanted to see it in writing that I can't have it."</span> They settle on the time Halo's reference leak allows, with the rationale written under it. The current layout doesn't meet it, but some layout can, and that's the design's problem to solve.`,
          why:`Feasible means it can be achieved within physics, technology, cost and schedule, by some design. Use the data to agree a value, and keep the rationale with the requirement.`},
        {t:`Mark the value to be determined until the layout is drawn and measured.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The layout gets designed without a target, and then the target is set to whatever the layout achieved.`,
          why:`A requirement that waits for the design describes the design. It can't drive it.`},
        {t:`Write 60 seconds as a goal and the reference leak time as the requirement.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`Helen is satisfied. The goal floats there with no owner and no verification, and engineers argue about which number counts.`,
          why:`Separating goals from requirements can work, if it's explicit how each is used. Here it mostly creates two numbers for one thing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s7`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Same job`, q:`Beto laughs at a verification column that says test on every line. How should Nadia set the methods?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday she met Beto Salinas for coffee in Castle Rock, which Beto called halfway and wasn't. He'd been her bench partner at the avionics shop in Colorado Springs, and he was still there. He took her laptop without asking, scrolled to the verification column, and laughed out loud.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Beto">"Test. Test. Test. Test,"</span> he read. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"You still love a green PASS, Brooks."</span> He knew why. He'd never once asked her about it, which was one of the reasons they were still friends. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"Same job, different hardware,"</span> he said, and pushed the laptop back across the table. <span class="said" data-who="Beto">"So do it the way you used to."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep test on every line, the strongest evidence for a crewed module.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Tiago prices it Monday. Testing fifteen years of micrometeoroids, and every warning label, isn't strong evidence. It's impossible or silly.`,
          why:`Test is one of four methods, not the best one everywhere. Some things can only be analyzed, and some are proved by looking.`},
        {t:`Go line by line and pick the method that gives real evidence for each.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They do sixty lines over two refills. Labels go to inspection, the hatch handle to demonstration, leak rate to test, and fifteen years of debris to analysis. Beto steals the last of her muffin as his fee.`,
          why:`Each requirement gets the verification method that actually produces the evidence it needs: inspection, analysis, demonstration, or test. Assigning methods as you write the requirement keeps the requirement verifiable.`},
        {t:`Move most lines to analysis and keep test for the safety items.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`It looks cheap on paper. Then Tiago asks what model she plans to use to show the noise at a pillow.`,
          why:`Picking methods by cost alone puts analysis where only a measurement will do. Match the method to the requirement.`},
        {t:`Leave the methods blank for Tiago to fill in with his test plan.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Tiago will do a good job. He'll also find thirty requirements he can't verify at all, months after they were written.`,
          why:`Asking the test lead is smart. Waiting means you find unverifiable requirements after the baseline instead of before.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s8`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T9`, title:`Fifteen years of gravel`, q:`The shielding requirement holds for fifteen years in orbit. Which verification approach fits it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`One requirement made Tiago put his coffee down. The shielding had to keep the probability of a penetrating micrometeoroid or debris strike below a set value over fifteen years in orbit.`,`Then he picked it back up and grinned. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"There's a light gas gun in Ohio. It shoots panels, not years."</span> The question was what to call the rest of the proof.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use analysis with the debris model, anchored by impact tests on panels.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`The rest is analysis, Nadia says, with the debris model checked against real shots. Tiago books the gas gun for a dozen sample panels. The shots calibrate the model, and the model covers fifteen years. <span class="said" data-who="Tiago">"I can stand behind that,"</span> he says.`,
          why:`When the full condition can't be tested, analysis is the method, and tests on samples give it real data to stand on. Most hard requirements are verified by a combination like this.`},
        {t:`Test a full shield section at the impact lab and treat it as the proof.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:2,health:0},
          after:`The panel test is real data. A handful of shots doesn't show fifteen years of random strikes over the whole module.`,
          why:`Testing samples is valuable evidence. On its own it doesn't cover the requirement's full time span and environment. That's analysis.`},
        {t:`Inspect the shield's certificates, thickness and drawing at delivery.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The shield is exactly as drawn. Whether as drawn is enough is the question nobody answered.`,
          why:`Inspection confirms the part matches the design. It doesn't show the design meets a performance requirement.`},
        {t:`Rewrite the requirement as a shield thickness the shop can measure.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`Now it's easy to verify and doesn't say what the crew needs. Thickness is a design answer, not a protection level.`,
          why:`Don't bend a requirement to fit a convenient verification method. Keep the real need and choose the method that can prove it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s9`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M6`, title:`Everyone wants the keys`, q:`Halo's program office wants to fix wording in FRO's model directly. What access should Halo get?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halo's program office called Monday of week six. They wanted edit access to FRO's requirements in the model before the review, so their engineers could fix wording directly instead of sending comments.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Amara">"It would save a week of email,"</span> Amara said. <span class="said" data-who="Amara">"Your call."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Give Halo edit access to the requirements in the model before the review.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`By Thursday nine requirements have changed and FRO can't say who changed them, or when, or why.`,
          why:`Information management decides who gets what access to which information, and keeps a record. Open edit rights for an outside party mean the program's own requirements no longer say who changed what, or why.`},
        {t:`Email Halo a weekly PDF and collect their comments in a spreadsheet.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It's safe. The PDFs are out of date by the time Halo reads them, and the comments point at line numbers that moved.`,
          why:`Protecting the source is right. Sending stale copies creates its own version problem.`},
        {t:`Give Halo read access to the live set with a comment tool on each line.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Nadia gives Halo read access to the live set and a comment box on every line, but not the pen. Halo sees the current set every day and comments on the line itself. FRO's people make the edits, and every one has a name and a reason. Halo's lead engineer says it's better than what they asked for.`,
          why:`Information management gives each stakeholder the right access to current information, and keeps changes controlled and recorded. Write the rule down so it holds after this week.`},
        {t:`Keep Halo out of the set until it's baselined at the review.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Halo walks into the review seeing the set for the first time, with forty comments they could have sent in week four.`,
          why:`Locking stakeholders out doesn't protect the requirements. It just moves their input to the worst possible moment.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s10`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M5`, title:`Three obvious things`, q:`The set froze for review Tuesday, and Wes has three lines he wants added. What should Nadia do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The set froze for review on Tuesday of week six. On Wednesday Wes dropped a sticky note on Nadia's keyboard with three lines in his square capitals. Hatch handle on both sides, one hand, no tools. Seat track down both sides of the aisle for stowage. All harnesses in the overhead channel.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Obvious stuff,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Every cargo module had them. Just put them in."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Add the three lines to the frozen set before Thursday's review.`, s:0, flag:`acceptedwesreqs`, d:{trust:4,conf:-3,health:-6},
          after:`Three lines go in without a parent, an impact check, or a number anyone assigned on purpose. Wes is happy. The set the reviewers read is no longer the set that was frozen.`,
          why:`Once the set is frozen for the baseline, changes go through change control, however obvious. Obvious requirements still have costs, interfaces and side effects someone has to look at.`},
        {t:`Write each one up as a change request with Wes's rationale.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia writes each of his lines up as a change request and leaves the reason blank for him. Wes looks at the three forms and then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Wes">"Paperwork."</span> He fills in the rationale anyway, two lines each, and they're good lines. The requests go into the queue with numbers.`,
          why:`Configuration management controls changes to a baselined or frozen set. A change request captures the proposal and the reason, so it can be assessed and decided, not slipped in.`},
        {t:`Tell Wes the set is frozen and leave the three lines out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Wes stops bringing her things. The three rules don't go away. They just stop coming through her.`,
          why:`Freezing a baseline doesn't mean refusing changes. It means changes have a path. Without one, they go around you.`},
        {t:`List them in the review package as open issues for the reviewers.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`They're visible, which is good. The reviewers have no impact analysis, so they spend forty minutes guessing and then defer them.`,
          why:`Surfacing the change is right. A review isn't the place to assess it from scratch. Do the assessment first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s11`, domain:`Technical management`, task:`M5`, title:`What it touches`, q:`Amara's first configuration control board meets Friday. What should Nadia bring it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[[`Amara set up the first configuration control board for Friday after the review. Wes's three lines were already in the set, and she wanted the board to look at them anyway, after the fact. She asked Nadia what the board would need to decide.`,`Amara set up the first configuration control board for Friday after the review. Wes's three change requests were the first items on it. She asked Nadia what the board would need to decide.`,`Amara set up the first configuration control board for Friday after the review. Wes's three lines weren't on it. Nobody had written them up. She asked Nadia what the board would need if they ever got there.`,`Amara set up the first configuration control board for Friday after the review. Wes's three items were on it, deferred from the review with no analysis behind them. She asked Nadia what the board would need to decide.`][(s.picks&&s.picks[9]!==undefined)?s.picks[9]:((s.flags.acceptedwesreqs||G.acceptedwesreqs)?0:1)],`Wes, passing, said the board would need about thirty seconds.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Each item's cost and schedule impact, for Amara to weigh against budget.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The costs are small and the board approves all three. The seat track takes width out of the aisle that quarter four has to walk, and it isn't on anyone's sheet.`,
          why:`Cost and schedule matter. An impact assessment also covers technical effects, interfaces, and the other requirements a change touches.`},
        {t:`Wes's own recommendation on each of the three design rules he wrote.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The board takes thirty seconds, as promised. The overhead harness rule quietly runs through two interfaces nobody has defined yet.`,
          why:`Expert judgment is an input, not an assessment. The board decides on impacts, and somebody has to find them first.`},
        {t:`An impact trace of each change across everything linked to it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia wants the board to see everything each change touches, so Jun runs the trace. The hatch handle maps cleanly to a need. The seat track narrows the aisle by eleven centimeters, and the egress model gets slower. The overhead harness touches two interfaces nobody has defined yet. The board has something real to decide.`,
          why:`Impact assessment uses traceability to find everything a change touches: other requirements, interfaces, performance, cost, schedule and verification. Then the board decides with eyes open.`},
        {t:`Approval of all three now, with the impact work done during design.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`They're approved. The assessment during design finds the aisle problem, which is now a change to an approved change.`,
          why:`Approving first and assessing later is backwards. The assessment is what makes the decision a decision.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f3s12`, domain:`Technical`, task:`T3`, title:`What goes in the baseline`, q:`Jun's trace report shows eleven requirements still marked to be determined. What should happen at the review?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The night before the review Jun's trace report came to three hundred and ninety six requirements. Eleven still had values marked to be determined, including the egress layout's test conditions.`,`Wes wanted the review pushed until the open values were closed. Amara wanted to baseline it and stop talking about it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Push the review back until every one of the eleven TBDs is closed.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Two of the eleven depend on the trade study in five weeks. The review moves twice and design starts with no baseline at all.`,
          why:`Waiting for zero open values can stall a program. A baseline can carry tracked open items, and the alternative is designing to nothing.`},
        {t:`Baseline it as is and let design resolve the open TBDs as it goes.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The baseline is set. The eleven TBDs have no owner, so each one gets resolved by whichever designer touches it first.`,
          why:`An open value with no owner and no date isn't tracked. It's a gap with a label on it.`},
        {t:`Baseline it with an owner and a closure date on every open TBD.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Nadia sides with neither. They baseline it, with an owner and a closing date on every open value. The review package lists all eleven on one page: who, by when, and from what analysis. The reviewers ask about three of them, and all three have answers.`,
          why:`The requirements baseline is set at the review and controlled after it. Open values are acceptable when each is tracked with an owner and a closure plan, so the baseline stays honest.`},
        {t:`Pull the eleven open requirements and add them back by change request.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The baseline is clean and missing eleven things the module needs. Adding them back costs eleven change requests in the busiest month.`,
          why:`It keeps the baseline tidy, at the cost of hiding real requirements. Tracking open values in place is usually better than pulling them out.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f3s3`, title:`Ready for review?`, setup:[`Jun's first full trace report covers 400 system requirements. 388 trace up to a parent. 372 have a verification method. 14 have a value marked to be determined. Amara's entry criteria for the review are every requirement traced up, every requirement with a method, and no more than 5 percent open values.`,`Percent is the count divided by the total, times 100. After the report, Jun finds parents for 8 of the orphans, and the team agrees the other orphans aren't necessary and deletes them.`],
      fields:[{label:`Orphan requirements (no parent)`, answer:12, tol:0},{label:`Percent with a verification method`, answer:93, tol:0},{label:`Percent with a value to be determined`, answer:3.5, tol:0.01},{label:`Requirements left after the orphans are fixed or deleted`, answer:396, tol:0}],
      solution:[`Orphans: 400 minus 388 = 12 with no parent.`,`Methods: 372 / 400 = 0.93, or 93 percent. That fails the entry criteria until the other 28 get methods.`,`Open values: 14 / 400 = 0.035, or 3.5 percent, inside the 5 percent limit.`,`Of the 12 orphans, 8 get parents and 12 minus 8 = 4 are deleted. 400 minus 4 = 396 requirements, all traced.`,`An orphan isn't automatically wrong. Sometimes the parent was missed. When nobody can say why a requirement exists, though, it probably isn't necessary, and every unnecessary requirement costs design, verification and money.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f3s8`, title:`What's wrong, and how would you prove it?`, intro:`The first five are HEARTH draft requirements with one main flaw each. Pick the characteristic it breaks. The last four are fixed requirements. Pick the verification method.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`"The cabin shall be quiet."`, choices:[`Singular`,`Verifiable`,`Feasible`,`Appropriate`], answer:1, why:`There's no measure, no location and no condition, so there's no way to show it was met.`},
      {prompt:`"The hatch shall open within 10 seconds and shall seal to the leak limit."`, choices:[`Necessary`,`Feasible`,`Singular`,`Unambiguous`], answer:2, why:`Two behaviors in one requirement. It can half pass, which means it can't be verified cleanly. Split it.`},
      {prompt:`"The module shall have zero air leakage for its fifteen year life."`, choices:[`Feasible`,`Singular`,`Conforming`,`Necessary`], answer:0, why:`Every pressure vessel leaks a little. Zero can't be achieved, so the requirement needs an achievable limit.`},
      {prompt:`"The crew quarters shall be adequately sized."`, choices:[`Singular`,`Necessary`,`Unambiguous`,`Feasible`], answer:2, why:`Adequate means something different to every reader. State dimensions or volume with conditions.`},
      {prompt:`A requirement for a stowage rail type that no stakeholder requirement or analysis calls for, added because the last program had it.`, choices:[`Verifiable`,`Necessary`,`Singular`,`Feasible`], answer:1, why:`If nobody can say what breaks without it, it may not be necessary. Trace would show it as an orphan.`},
      {prompt:`Warning labels shall be present on both faces of the hatch.`, choices:[`Analysis`,`Test`,`Inspection`,`Demonstration`], answer:2, why:`Presence and markings are confirmed by looking. That's inspection.`},
      {prompt:`Probability of no penetrating debris strike over fifteen years shall be at least the specified value.`, choices:[`Test`,`Analysis`,`Demonstration`,`Inspection`], answer:1, why:`Fifteen years of random strikes can't be tested. Analysis, anchored by sample tests, is the method.`},
      {prompt:`One crew member shall be able to open the hatch from either side without tools.`, choices:[`Demonstration`,`Inspection`,`Analysis`,`Test`], answer:0, why:`You operate it and watch it work, pass or fail, with no instrumented measurement needed. That's demonstration.`},
      {prompt:`Cabin leak rate at nominal pressure shall not exceed the specified limit.`, choices:[`Inspection`,`Demonstration`,`Test`,`Analysis`], answer:2, why:`A measured value under controlled conditions is a test.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which process turns stakeholder requirements into a technical view of what the system itself must do?`, opts:[`System requirements definition`,`Stakeholder needs and requirements definition`,`System architecture definition`,`Business or mission analysis`], a:0, why:`System requirements definition takes the user's view and produces verifiable requirements on the system, which architecture definition uses next.`, task:`T3`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`"The pump shall start within 2 seconds and shall run for 10,000 hours." Which characteristic does this requirement break?`, opts:[`Feasible`,`Singular`,`Necessary`,`Appropriate`], a:1, why:`Two behaviors in one requirement means it can't pass or fail cleanly. Split it into two singular requirements.`, task:`T3`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A requirement demands zero leakage from a pressure vessel for fifteen years. Which characteristic is most in doubt?`, opts:[`Singular`,`Conforming`,`Feasible`,`Verifiable`], a:2, why:`No real pressure vessel has zero leakage. A requirement that can't be achieved within physics and technology isn't feasible.`, task:`T3`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`When is the system requirements baseline usually established?`, opts:[`At the critical design review`,`At the start of verification testing`,`At the preliminary design review`,`At the system requirements review`], a:3, why:`The system requirements review approves the requirements set as a baseline, and configuration management controls it from then on. Some programs set the functional baseline a little later, at a system functional or system design review, but of these choices the SRR is the one.`, task:`T3`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`Which verification method is used when a requirement can't be fully tested, such as performance over a fifteen year life?`, opts:[`Analysis`,`Inspection`,`Demonstration`,`Test`], a:0, why:`Analysis uses calculation, models and simulation, often backed by test data on samples, to verify what can't be tested in full.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`A crew member opens a hatch without tools while an inspector watches and records pass or fail, with no measurements taken. Which method is this?`, opts:[`Inspection`,`Demonstration`,`Analysis`,`Qualification test`], a:1, why:`Demonstration operates the system and observes that it works, without instrumented measurement. Measuring under controlled conditions would make it a test.`, task:`T9`, domain:`Technical`},
    {q:`After the requirements baseline is set, the chief engineer wants to add a requirement he says is obvious. What should happen?`, opts:[`Add it now, since it's obviously correct`,`Wait to add it until the next review`,`Submit a change request for assessment`,`Put it straight into the design notes`], a:2, why:`Changes to a baseline go through configuration control: a change request, an impact assessment, and a board decision, however obvious the change seems.`, task:`M5`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`What should the impact assessment for a requirements change cover?`, opts:[`The cost of the change alone`,`The requester's own rationale`,`The approver's schedule for it`,`Effects on all it touches`], a:3, why:`An impact assessment uses traceability to find effects on other requirements, interfaces, performance, verification, cost and schedule, so the board can decide.`, task:`M5`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A customer wants to edit the supplier's requirements directly. Which approach best fits information management?`, opts:[`Read access and a comment tool, with edits by the owner`,`Full edit rights for the customer, to save some time`,`Weekly PDF copies, with the comments kept in a spreadsheet`,`No access at all for the customer until after review`], a:0, why:`Information management gives stakeholders the right access to current information while keeping changes controlled and recorded.`, task:`M6`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`A trace report shows a requirement with no parent and no source anyone can name. What does this most likely suggest?`, opts:[`It's a gap in coverage`,`It may not be necessary`,`It needs a test method`,`It must be a safety item`], a:1, why:`A requirement with no parent is an orphan. Unless a missed parent turns up, it may be unnecessary. A gap is the opposite: a need with no requirement under it.`, task:`X3`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Where should safety requirements for a crewed system mainly come from?`, opts:[`The chief engineer's experience`,`The customer's contract terms`,`Hazard analysis, traced to hazards`,`The test lead's verification plan`], a:2, why:`Safety requirements come from hazard analysis and control specific hazards, and each should trace to the hazard it addresses.`, task:`X2`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`What does a technical review such as the system requirements review mainly do?`, opts:[`Approve the budget for the next stage of work`,`Replace the need for a later design review`,`Hand the program to the customer for approval`,`Give evidence for a decision about proceeding`], a:3, why:`Technical reviews assess the work against entry and exit criteria and give evidence to the decision gate that decides whether to proceed.`, task:`X1`, domain:`Cross-cutting`},
    {q:`Where are the planned technical reviews and their entry and exit criteria usually set out?`, opts:[`In the SEMP and project plans`,`In the interface control documents`,`In the requirements themselves`,`In the risk register`], a:0, why:`Project planning produces the plans, including the SEMP, that define which reviews will be held and what each needs to start and finish.`, task:`M1`, domain:`Technical management`},
    {q:`Which process provides the organization's standard life cycle processes that each project then tailors?`, opts:[`Infrastructure management`,`Life cycle model management`,`Project assessment and control`,`Portfolio management`], a:1, why:`Life cycle model management defines, maintains and improves the organization's processes and models. Each project tailors from that standard.`, task:`O1`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`},
    {q:`A chief engineer's design rules exist only in his memory. Which process aims to capture that kind of knowledge so others can reuse it?`, opts:[`Human resource management`,`Configuration management`,`Knowledge management`,`Information management`], a:2, why:`Knowledge management captures and shares the organization's knowledge, including expertise that lives in people, so it can be reused across projects.`, task:`O6`, domain:`Agreement and organizational`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The night before the review Nadia was still at her desk at nine when her phone lit up.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Dad" time="9:12 PM">BIG DAY TOMORROW. ROSIE DREW YOU A NEW ROCKET. 5 IN THE WINDOW NOW. SHE PUT YOU IN. CALLIE SAYS YOU MISSED SUNDAY. SHE WONT SAY IT SO IM SAYING IT.</prop>`,
    `She read it at her desk with the review package open on the other screen. She typed an apology and deleted it. What she sent was shorter: she'd call Callie Sunday, and Rosie should save her the window seat. Then she put Sunday at noon in her calendar with an alarm on it, and sat there a minute with nothing to check.`,
    `The review ran all of Thursday in the big conference room. Helen was on the screen from Halo, and an observer from OCSC, the federal office that audits human rating, sat in the back writing in a notebook nobody got to see. Jun presented the trace. Tiago presented the verification methods. Odette walked through the hazard controls without once raising her voice.`,
    (s.score>=28
      ?`At four the chair read the result: proceed, with four action items. The requirements baseline was set. Helen said, on the screen, that it was the first requirements set she'd read that sounded like it had met a crew. Amara shook Nadia's hand in the hallway and didn't say anything about the schedule, which was new.`
      :`At four the chair read the result: proceed, with fourteen action items, most of them wording. The requirements baseline was set anyway. Amara looked at the action list, then at Nadia, and said they'd talk Monday.`),
    [`Wes's three lines were in the baselined set, with no parent and no change request behind them. They read like everything else, and the review never asked where they came from.`,`Wes's three change requests sat in the board's queue for Friday, numbered, with his rationale in his square capitals. Outside the baseline, where they belonged until somebody decided.`,`Wes's three lines weren't in the set and weren't in the queue. He hadn't brought them up again, and she hadn't asked.`,`Wes's three items sat on Friday's agenda, deferred from the review, with no analysis behind them yet.`][(s.picks&&s.picks[9]!==undefined)?s.picks[9]:((s.flags.acceptedwesreqs||G.acceptedwesreqs)?0:1)],
    `Friday morning Nadia went to the structures area to borrow the latest hatch drawing. It was on Wes's plotter, still warm, revision C. She read the notes block out of habit.`,
    `<prop say="Notes seven, eight and nine on revision C of the hatch and aisle drawing. Hatch handle on both sides, one hand, no tools. Seat track on both sides of the aisle, full length. All harness routed in the overhead channel. Drawn by W. Harland, Monday of week six." type="doc" title="HEARTH primary structure, hatch and aisle, rev C, notes block (excerpt)">7. HATCH HANDLE BOTH SIDES. ONE HAND. NO TOOLS.<br>8. SEAT TRACK BOTH SIDES OF AISLE, FULL LENGTH.<br>9. ALL HARNESS ROUTED IN OVERHEAD CHANNEL.<br>Drawn: W. Harland. Date: Wk 6 Mon.</prop>`,
    `Three notes, drawn into the structure before anybody had decided them. The aisle on the drawing was already eleven centimeters narrower than the aisle on the tape.`,
    `The baseline was fourteen hours old. Wes's drawing was three days older.`,
    ((s.picks&&s.picks[9]===2)
      ?`Nobody was going to decide it at all. Nadia rolled the sheet under her arm and walked it over to him.`
      :`The board met at ten to decide whether Wes could have what he'd already drawn. Nadia rolled the sheet under her arm and walked it over to him first.`)
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch03.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Shape of the System`
});
